Feature: dalogin Servlet request DTOs and response helpers

Contract IDs
- None (internal Servlet structure; all existing `/login/*` HTTP contracts are preserved)

Intent
- Extract request parsing and response construction from every class in
  `dalogin-quarkus/src/main/java/com/dalogin/servlets`.
- Keep all 14 endpoints as Undertow `@WebServlet` classes.
- Use immutable endpoint request DTOs for complex request processing.
- Keep trivial pass-through request reads direct; do not wrap them in ceremonial DTOs.
- Use explicit endpoint response helpers for both complex and straightforward responses, backed by
  one shared mechanical JSON writer.
- Implement this work in the same production-code pass as datasource legacy removal, CDI injection,
  SQLAccess family splitting, and final caller migration.

Implementation model
- `speckit.system.dalogin-persistence-servlet-adaptation.implementation-example.md`
- `HelloWorld` is the complete reference slice. Other Servlets apply the same boundaries while
  preserving their own request and response contracts.

Execution plan
- `speckit.system.dalogin-persistence-servlet-adaptation.plan`

Context
- JAX-RS `@BeanParam`, `@FormParam`, `@HeaderParam`, `@Consumes`, and `@Produces` do not bind
  `HttpServlet.doGet`/`doPost` arguments.
- Custom response annotations would recreate JAX-RS response mapping behind less familiar
  infrastructure.
- The owner decided to keep Servlets, use immutable request DTO parsing for complex request
  processing, and explicit response helpers across all Servlet endpoints.
- Current source contains 14 Servlet classes. Ten directly read headers/parameters; the others still
  construct responses and therefore participate in response-helper extraction.
- The 14 classes declare 26 `doGet`/`doPost` handlers. Ten classes also declare `init()` and
  `destroy()`; seven `init()` methods initialize `AesUtil`, three are empty, and all ten `destroy()`
  methods are no-ops. The migration unit is the entire class, not only the POST branch that calls
  SQLAccess.
- `AesUtil` holds a mutable `javax.crypto.Cipher` field. `Cipher` is not thread-safe, while each
  Servlet instance serves concurrent requests. The seven per-Servlet `AesUtil` fields and the same
  pattern in `ChangePswFilter` are therefore not safe candidates for simple shared CDI injection.

Servlet scope

| Servlet | Request handling | Response helper direction |
|---|---|---|
| `HelloWorld` | complex: `LoginRequest` | login |
| `Registration` | complex: registration DTO(s) | registration |
| `RegistrationWithoutVoucher` | complex: registration DTO(s) | registration where wire-identical |
| `Voucher` | simple single voucher read; no DTO | voucher |
| `RegActivation` | simple single header read; no DTO | activation |
| `ChangePassword` | complex: password-reset DTO | password reset |
| `ChangePasswordCode` | complex: confirmation-code DTO | password reset |
| `ChangePasswordNewPassword` | complex: new-password DTO | password reset |
| `AdminServlet` | simple optional session-id read; no DTO | admin |
| `ManagePurchases` | simple operation-specific reads; no DTO unless inventory disproves | purchase |
| `GetAllPurchases` | no direct input DTO | purchase |
| `CheckOut` | no direct input DTO | checkout |
| `LoggingOut` | no direct input DTO | logout |
| `ActiveSessionsServlet` | no direct input DTO | active sessions |

Request-complexity rule
- **Complex request processing** maps a coherent input model from multiple values, mixes headers and
  parameters, applies cross-field validation/HMAC input ordering, trims/defaults several fields, or
  performs type conversion. It gets an immutable DTO and factory.
- **Simple request access** reads at most a small operation-specific value set and passes it through
  without cross-field validation, normalization policy, or conversion. It stays explicit in the
  Servlet; a DTO would add indirection without modeling anything.
- **Response construction is different.** Even a straightforward response represents a wire
  contract (status, content type, field names/types, headers/cookies), so it goes through a named
  response helper. The helper may be one line over shared mechanics; it does not need a matching DTO.

Relationship to SQLAccess refactor
- This feature and the three SQLAccess structural features form one implementation pass:
  `dalogin-datasource-legacy-removal`, `dalogin-sqlaccess-cdi-injection`,
  `dalogin-sqlaccess-family-split`, and this feature.
- Each Servlet MUST be edited once into its final shape: final family-bean injection, final
  data-access calls, DTO parsing where request processing is complex, and response-helper delegation.
- No intermediate monolithic injected SQLAccess caller shape is required.
- The module may remain uncompilable during the pass. The first compile occurs only after all
  production code in all four features has been migrated.
- `dalogin-login-logout-error-handling` remains separate because it intentionally changes behavior.

Functional requirements
- `FR-001`: All 14 endpoint classes MUST remain `HttpServlet` implementations with unchanged
  `@WebServlet` names and URL patterns.
- `FR-002`: The solution MUST NOT introduce JAX-RS resources, `@BeanParam`, Bean Validation,
  CDI request-scoped mutable holders, custom response annotations, or a new dependency.
- `FR-003`: Every complex Servlet request contract MUST have an immutable request DTO. Complexity is
  determined by the Request-complexity rule above, not merely by the presence of request input.
- `FR-003a`: Request DTOs MUST be Java records. Structured response data SHOULD also use records
  where that clarifies the contract; constant one-off error bodies do not require ceremonial
  response records.
- `FR-004`: A request DTO factory MUST be the only place in that complex request flow that calls
  `getHeader` or `getParameter`.
- `FR-005`: A Servlet with materially different GET and POST contracts MAY use separate DTOs.
  Unrelated fields MUST NOT be combined into one nullable field bag.
- `FR-006`: A no-input or simple pass-through request flow MUST NOT receive an empty or ceremonial
  DTO for symmetry. Its small direct reads remain in the Servlet.
- `FR-007`: DTOs own wire-name mapping, baseline trimming/defaults, required-value detection, and
  safe type conversion. They MUST NOT own CDI, sessions, responses, SQL, client calls, crypto, or
  logging.
- `FR-008`: Every Servlet response branch, including straightforward `sendError`, redirect, and
  small JSON branches, MUST delegate response construction to an endpoint-specific response helper.
- `FR-009`: One shared stateless `ServletResponses` support type MAY own mechanical content-type,
  encoding, status, and writer handling. It MUST NOT choose endpoint statuses or payload fields.
- `FR-010`: Response helpers MAY be shared only where wire contracts are field-for-field identical.
  Similar business domains alone are insufficient.
- `FR-011`: Servlets retain orchestration, session/request-attribute mutation, data-access calls,
  downstream client calls, authentication, crypto, redirects, and response-branch selection.
- `FR-012`: Existing status codes, content types, encodings, redirects, JSON field names,
  capitalization, value types, response headers, and cookie properties MUST remain unchanged.
- `FR-013`: Each terminal response is written exactly once. No Servlet writes again after a helper
  commits a terminal response.
- `FR-014`: Existing malformed/missing input outcomes MUST be captured before extraction and
  preserved, even where the baseline outcome is imperfect.
- `FR-015`: No Servlet may retain direct `JSONObject` construction or `PrintWriter` ownership after
  migration. Non-JSON streaming is exempt only when explicitly documented by the endpoint inventory.
- `FR-016`: The work MUST land in the same single pass as the SQLAccess refactor. No compile, test,
  or reviewable compatibility shim occurs between the four structural feature phases.
- `FR-017`: For each Servlet, final family-bean injection and DTO/helper migration MUST happen in
  one edit so the caller is not migrated twice.
- `FR-018`: `HelloWorld` follows the full reference example, including its ten request values,
  presence-based `ios`, default `M`, and exact mobile/web response differences.
- `FR-019`: `HelloWorld`, `Registration`, and `RegistrationWithoutVoucher` MUST treat a newly
  created `HttpSession` as pending while obtaining the session ID/time needed by persistence. They
  MUST NOT publish `user`, `deviceId`, token, or active-session attributes before their required
  persistence work succeeds.
- `FR-020`: Device insertion, session insertion, and token retrieval for normal login and
  registration-without-voucher MUST execute as one transaction in
  `DeviceSessionManager.establishLogin`. Registration-with-voucher continues to use the atomic
  `AccountManager.wrapUpRegistration` transaction.
- `FR-021`: If required persistence fails in any session-establishing flow, its transaction MUST
  roll back, the pending `HttpSession` MUST be invalidated, and a 5xx response MUST be returned.
- `FR-022`: After `establishLogin` commits, `HelloWorld` MAY publish session attributes and write
  cookies, headers, and the success response.
- `FR-023`: The 100 ms token retry MUST be removed. Token retrieval runs on the same transaction
  connection after `insert_sessionCreated`, so missing token data is a persistence failure rather
  than an asynchronous-commit condition.
- `FR-024`: `RegActivation` MUST NOT create an `HttpSession` solely to obtain `ServletContext` for
  SQLAccess. CDI removes that need; activation uses its injected final family bean directly.
- `FR-025`: The migration unit is the whole Servlet class. Every declared `doGet`, `doPost`,
  `init`, `destroy`, and private helper involved in request/response or persistence flow MUST be
  reviewed and moved to the final structure in the same edit.
- `FR-026`: All 26 request-handler methods MUST be covered. GET paths MUST receive the same
  request-complexity classification and response-helper treatment as POST paths.
- `FR-027`: Empty `init()` and no-op `destroy()` overrides MUST be removed. Non-empty `init()`
  behavior MUST NOT be silently dropped.
- `FR-028`: Servlets MUST inject final persistence family beans, never `DataSource`, `Connection`,
  `DBConnectionManager`, or a replacement SQLAccess facade. Family beans alone inject `DataSource`.
- `FR-029`: Private Servlet helpers MUST be reviewed with their handler. Request parsing and response
  construction move to records/helpers; business orchestration stays in clearly named Servlet
  helpers where extraction would mix HTTP and persistence concerns.
- `FR-030`: The seven Servlet `AesUtil` initialization paths and `ChangePswFilter` MUST migrate to
  an injected stateless `CryptoService`. It MUST create/use a request-local `Cipher` (directly or
  through a per-operation `AesUtil`) and MUST NOT share the current mutable `Cipher` across threads.
- `FR-031`: Removing `init()` MUST preserve key size, iteration count, salts, IVs, passphrases,
  ciphertext format, and every successful encryption/decryption result.

Non-functional requirements
- `NFR-001`: Request DTOs are immutable and hold no shared mutable state.
- `NFR-002`: Response helpers and shared response support are stateless.
- `NFR-003`: No password, HMAC, token, confirmation code, voucher, or decrypted device identifier is
  newly logged or included in an exception.
- `NFR-004`: Request/response types live outside persistence packages.
- `NFR-005`: Common code is extracted only for actual mechanics or identical contracts; abstraction
  MUST NOT erase endpoint-specific behavior.

Out of scope
- Converting any Servlet to JAX-RS.
- Deliberately correcting unrelated status codes, error payloads, logout behavior, or token
  rotation. The normal-login persistence failure and session-publication changes in `FR-019`-
  `FR-023` are explicitly in scope.
- Applying the pattern to filters/listeners unless they construct an HTTP response already in the
  combined caller migration.
- Generalizing a response framework beyond dalogin's current Servlet endpoints.

Open questions
- None. Servlet-wide scope and combination with the SQLAccess refactor are owner-decided.

Acceptance criteria
- `AC-001`: All 14 Servlet mappings and names are unchanged.
- `AC-002`: Complex request flows contain no direct `getHeader`/`getParameter` calls; simple
  pass-through reads remain explicit and no ceremonial DTO exists.
- `AC-003`: The baseline inventory classifies every request flow as complex, simple, or no-input,
  with a reason; DTO coverage matches the complex set exactly.
- `AC-004`: No Servlet directly constructs `JSONObject` or owns a `PrintWriter`, except a documented
  non-JSON streaming case.
- `AC-005`: Every response branch maps to an endpoint helper and retains exact baseline wire output.
- `AC-006`: Shared `ServletResponses` contains mechanics only and no endpoint field/status choices.
- `AC-007`: DTOs and helpers contain no persistence, CDI, session, client, or crypto behavior.
- `AC-008`: Each Servlet injects only its final SQLAccess family beans; no monolithic/facade caller
  shape remains.
- `AC-009`: The repository has no reviewable or compiled intermediate state between datasource
  removal, CDI injection, family split, and Servlet request/response extraction.
- `AC-010`: The first full compile after the combined pass succeeds after reported misses are fixed.
- `AC-011`: Targeted `LoginFlowTest` cases pass without `mbook`, `mbooks`, or Apache.
- `AC-012`: SQL text, parameter order, result mapping, Servlet mappings, and success-path HTTP
  contracts are unchanged except for explicit cleanup and the login transaction/publication
  behavior authorized by `FR-019`-`FR-023`.
- `AC-013`: `user` and `deviceId` are absent from pending login/registration sessions until their
  required persistence transaction successfully commits.
- `AC-014`: A forced failure in device insertion, session insertion, or token retrieval rolls back
  the normal-login transaction, invalidates the pending session, and returns 5xx.
- `AC-015`: The successful login path performs no sleep/retry and still returns the same tokens,
  cookies, header, and JSON payload.
- `AC-016`: `RegActivation` does not create a Servlet session solely for datasource access.
- `AC-017`: All 26 declared request handlers are accounted for in the migration inventory and no
  GET path remains in the old parsing/response style merely because POST was the primary flow.
- `AC-018`: All ten `init()` and ten no-op `destroy()` overrides are removed; the seven functional
  Servlet crypto paths and `ChangePswFilter` use injected thread-safe `CryptoService`.
- `AC-019`: Every complex request DTO is a Java record.
- `AC-020`: No Servlet injects or acquires `DataSource`/`Connection`; only final persistence family
  beans do.
- `AC-021`: No request-shared object contains a mutable `Cipher`; concurrent crypto operations
  preserve the existing ciphertext/decryption contract.

Owner-verified manually
- `MV-001`: Registration with and without voucher, activation, voucher, and password-reset flows.
- `MV-002`: Logout, admin, active sessions, checkout, and purchase flows.
- `MV-003`: Existing iOS and web clients receive unchanged response shapes, redirects, headers, and
  cookies.
